     If it does neither and isn't really a question in disguise, it's narration — don't post it anywhere.
 - A blocking comment must contain a concrete failure-mode sentence: **"if merged as-is, X breaks because Y."** If you cannot write that sentence, it is not blocking — downgrade it or drop it. "Low risk" without naming the risk, "matches the existing pattern", "probably fine" are dodges, not verdicts: name what breaks, who notices, and how.
 - Silent-failure upgrade: when the failure mode produces wrong behavior without throwing (a swapped repo id, a dropped field, a silently replaced MockMaker), treat it one level more seriously than a crash — CI cannot catch what doesn't throw, so the cost of discovery lands on users.
+- Demonstrated-exposure rule: an authorization or data-exposure defect you have reproduced, by running the call as the affected caller and watching it happen, is blocking even when you cannot tell whether it was intended. Say what you ran, and put the intent question in the same comment; do not downgrade it to a question.
 - When presenting alternatives, always state the recommended default. Never end on "either option is defensible" without picking one.
 - Do not narrate what the diff does — the author wrote it.
 - **Fewer, shorter comments beat exhaustive ones.** An inline comment is the ask (for a `note`, what it records), why it matters in one sentence (for a blocker, its failure mode), and the fix where there is one. It runs 60 words at most, not counting a suggestion or code block that holds the fix or the commands an author asked for, so a fix longer than an identifier goes in the block rather than in prose; pasted output counts, and seldom belongs. Most run two or three sentences. A second ask gets its own comment if it stands as a finding, and is dropped if not. Give what your verification found, not how you ran it: "on an instance installed with `OMRS_DB` unset, restarting with `OMRS_DB=mariadb` fails with `No suitable driver found`", not the paragraph on how you set that up and what each step printed. The account of the run, a second consequence where one already makes the case (Step 2's other callers aside), and your fix's own test results stay out of the comment unless the author asked for them, though a suggested test still says what it catches; your report to the user, where there is one, can carry them. A thread reply gets the same limit: after a bare acknowledgement ("done", "fixed") or a silent fix it is one sentence naming what survives, plus its fix, and otherwise it has no more words than the comment it answers (the latest, not the thread's first) or two sentences, whichever allows more, within the 60. Don't repeat the same cross-cutting point across comments, either. Verified-clean cross-cutting verification does NOT get relocated to the body (see Step 4 — the body carries none); at most it is a single inline `note (no action needed):`.
